-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Texture Binding Failure**: On adapters without the `float32-filterable` feature, binding `r32float` or `rgba32float` textures without explicitly setting `sampleType: 'unfilterable-float'` causes pipeline creation failure [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Buffer Offset Alignment**: Storage buffer offsets must be multiples of `device.limits.minStorageBufferOffsetAlignment` (typically 256 bytes). Non-aligned offsets cause bind-group creation failure [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Ray Origin Calculation**: Using a single camera position for ray origins in orthographic projections causes rays to converge incorrectly. Rays must start from the unprojected near plane [S1, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Float32 Texture Binding:** On adapters without `float32-filterable`, `r32float` and `rgba32float` sources fail if `sampleType` is omitted. Must be set to `unfilterable-float` [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Storage Buffer Offset Alignment:** WebGPU requires offsets to be multiples of `minStorageBufferOffsetAlignment` (commonly 256). Non-aligned offsets cause bind-group creation failure [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Ray Origin Calculation:** Using a single camera position as the origin for all rays causes convergence in orthographic projections and ignores near-plane clipping. Rays must start from the unprojected near plane [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of ray samples per pixel. The use of fixed-step marching implies a constant number of samples per ray, determined by the step size and volume depth.
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Linear with respect to the volume data size. The renderer consumes the input buffers/textures directly.
-*   **Performance**: Demonstrated on a 40³ volume with 72 ray samples per view, achieving 30 Hz updates for field and derivative calculations without volume readback [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Reusable, graph-native integration with WebGPU; supports both buffer and texture backends; manual interpolation allows for consistent behavior across backends.
-*   **Limitations**: Requires WebGPU support; construction-time invariants limit dynamic reconfiguration of data structure; manual interpolation may be slower than hardware filtering if the hardware supports it.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of ray samples and volume depth. Fixed-step marching implies constant steps per ray.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the volume data size (buffer or texture).
+*   **Measured Performance:** 40³ showcase volume with 72 ray samples per view, 6³ glyph lattice, and 30 Hz field/derivative updates without volume readback [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Reusable, graph-native, supports both buffers and textures, manual trilinear interpolation, early opacity termination.
+*   **Limitations:** Fixed-step marching may be less efficient than adaptive methods. Requires WebGPU support.
+*   **When to Use:** Browser-based volume rendering of structured grid data (scalar, vector, hybrid) where GPU performance is critical.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When adaptive ray marching is required, or when running on hardware without WebGPU support.
 
 ## Background
-StructuredVolumeRenderer was introduced as part of the luma.gl library, specifically in PR #3104, to provide a reusable GPU primitive for volume rendering within a graph-native execution model [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to support the "Vector Field Lab" showcase, which includes animated Taylor–Green fields and sparse 3D glyphs [S1, unofficial]. The implementation emphasizes numerical verification and parity between buffer and texture backends [S1, unofficial].
+StructuredVolumeRenderer was introduced as part of the luma.gl library to provide a reusable, graph-native GPU primitive for volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to support the Vector Field Lab showcase, which includes animated Taylor–Green fields, divergence views, and curl glyphs [S1, unofficial]. The renderer is designed to be integrated into the luma.gl scene graph, exposing facades for forward, deferred, ray-tracing, and ray-marching rendering modes [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the ongoing development of WebGPU-based visualization tools in the visgl ecosystem.
 
